WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.301
The pricing equation.

2
00:00:01.581 --> 00:00:09.112
Every hosted model bills you per token, with separate prices for input and output, quoted in dollars per million tokens.

3
00:00:09.392 --> 00:00:19.120
The cost of one request is a dot product: input tokens times the input price, plus output tokens times the output price, divided by a million.

4
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:29.960
If you have a math background, this is the easiest lesson in the course, and also the one that saves the most money, because most cost mistakes are arithmetic people never did.

5
00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.355
Two properties of real price tables shape your design.

6
00:00:33.635 --> 00:00:36.323
First, output is more expensive than input.

7
00:00:36.603 --> 00:00:48.016
At time of writing, every current Claude tier charges five times as much per output token as per input token (see the pricing page ), and other providers show similar ratios.

8
00:00:48.296 --> 00:00:54.525
A feature that writes long answers costs more than one that reads long documents and replies briefly.

9
00:00:54.805 --> 00:00:56.640
Second, thinking costs output.

10
00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:04.920
On reasoning models, the tokens the model spends thinking are billed as output tokens even when you never see their text, so usage.

11
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:09.168
output_tokens can be several times the length of the visible answer.

12
00:01:09.448 --> 00:01:13.181
Always price from the usage field, never from the text you received.

13
00:01:13.461 --> 00:01:15.808
Third, tiers differ by large factors.

14
00:01:16.088 --> 00:01:18.285
At time of writing, Claude Opus 5.

15
00:01:18.565 --> 00:01:25.371
5 is listed at 4 dollars input and 20 dollars output per million tokens, and Claude Haiku 5.

16
00:01:25.651 --> 00:01:26.760
5 at 0.

17
00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:28.107
10 and 0.

18
00:01:28.387 --> 00:01:32.376
50 for prompts up to 100,000 tokens.

19
00:01:32.656 --> 00:01:34.235
That is a factor of 40.

20
00:01:34.515 --> 00:01:40.787
Routing a high-volume, easy task to the smaller tier is often the single biggest cost decision you make.
